Same repo, same refs, same operation — the two transports disagree completely.
Depends on the pull bug (see the ticket about pull silently not updating refs);
that one is the cause, this is what the CLI-fallback path does with it.

`lib/App/karr/Git.pm:361-377`. Observed on an identical diverged board:

    native pull rv=1  last_error=(none)                    # silently no-op
    cli    fetch rv=0  last_error=... ! [rejected] ... (non-fast-forward)

The CLI fallback is the documented transport for ssh-config/ProxyCommand
remotes, so this is not a corner case for anyone using a jump host. On that
path a diverged board makes **every writing command fail forever**:

    karr --dir b edit 1 -a "B2"
    -> 3x "git pull failed: ... (non-fast-forward)"
    -> Pull failed after 3 attempts ... at lib/App/karr/Cmd/Edit.pm line 144
    EXIT=1

`karr sync` fails identically, so there is no way to recover from inside karr.
The clone is bricked until someone runs raw git by hand.

Two backends, opposite outcomes on the same state: silent corruption on one,
total lockout on the other. Whatever the fix for the pull refspec is, it has to
land on both transports, and there should be a test that asserts the two agree.
